Item 10: the CDM byte budget (Marcelo). Measurement done; DECISION NEEDED on tightening.

No code change yet. Branch head d465423f (pushed). The M3 runs the item 8 D4 package
(silicon-10), which has the same gate.rs as the head.

QUESTION
The byte budget lets one compute command's CDM walk cover up to 65 x 64 KiB = 4,259,840
bytes (1,064,960 four-byte barrier records), about 130x the old 8192-record cap. How long does a
worst-case walk take, and how long does it hold its lock?

WHAT THE WALK HOLDS (code reading)
- gate::cdm() runs in Queue::submit_job() -> command::prepare(..).validate() -> check_compute(),
  for each compute command of a SUBMIT (up to MAX_COMMANDS_PER_SUBMISSION = 64). The caller
  holds the queue's mutex (file.rs: `queue.lock().submit(..)`) for the whole SUBMIT. The device
  lock (Shared::state) is NOT held, so the engine, the poll work and other clients' queues keep
  running.
- Each mmu::Vm::read_user() (one per 256-byte window) and each command::cover() ->
  Vm::covers_range() takes and drops that VM's GPUVM exec lock (lock_vm). The VM lock is
  therefore held per call, for microseconds, not across the walk.
- Per record: barriers and links cost only the window reads (one read per 256 bytes). A direct
  launch adds a program check that is cached per page. An indirect launch (24 or 36 bytes) adds
  an UNCACHED cover() of its 12-byte arguments, one VM lock and page walk per record.
- The walk does not check for signals.

MEASUREMENTS
1. Host benchmark (tools/cdm-gate-bench/bench.rs, kept with the results). It runs the actual
   gate.rs with modelled read_user/cover (a mutex, a 3-level lookup, dc civac per line, dsb,
   copy), on the M3's CPU. Per command: barriers 2.0-2.4 ms (7.3 ms on one unpinned run);
   indirect launches 1.9-4.3 ms. The model's per-call cost is 16-28 ns, so it understates the
   kernel badly (see 2).
2. Hardware (J516C, silicon-10 kernel). pb-idle-check gains a step "D<n>": one command buffer of
   n vkCmdDispatchIndirect calls (Honeykrisp encodes one CDM indirect launch per dispatch, about
   86 stream bytes each), with vkQueueSubmit timed in userspace and Prepared::validate() timed
   with kprobe/kretprobe:
     n         submit (ms)   validate (ms)
     1           0.09-0.19      0.034
     1000        7.7-9.0        7.67
     8000       51.8-54.1      51.76
     20000      110.8-113.2   110.74
     40000      224.1-240     224.01
     80000      refused at the 64-link budget ("rejecting CDM link budget record")
   kprobe counts for n=40000: read_user 13464, cover 41410, covers_range 41410. Per-call times
   with entry/return probes (n=8000): covers_range median 4.0 us (8283 calls, 36.0 ms total),
   read_user median 5.0 us (2695 calls, 13.0 ms). So validate() is almost entirely these calls:
   about 5.6 us per indirect dispatch.
3. Worst case derived from 2:
   - barriers only: 16,640 reads x 5 us = about 83 ms per command;
   - 24-byte indirect launches, arguments on distinct pages: 177,450 covers x 4 us + 16,640
     reads x 5 us = about 0.8 s per command;
   - one SUBMIT of 64 such compute commands: about 51 s in one ioctl (about 5.3 s with barrier
     streams);
   - the old 8192-record cap, worst (indirect): 8192 x 4 us + about 770 reads x 5 us = about
     37 ms per command, about 2.4 s per SUBMIT.
   - A legitimate Mesa stream at the budget (about 49,500 dispatches) takes about 280 ms per
     command today, because Mesa's dispatches point at the same arguments page and the cover()
     result is not cached.

ASSESSMENT
Not a device-wide stall: the device lock is not held, so the GPU and other clients run. But one
process can make a single uninterruptible SUBMIT ioctl take tens of seconds, roughly 20x the old
cap's worst case, holding its queue mutex, and with millions of lock/unlock cycles on its VM's
exec lock. I consider this unreasonable, so the budget should be tightened. A legitimate heavy
stream also spends nearly all of its 280 ms on repeated page checks of the same page.

PROPOSAL (needs your call; the trade-off is legitimate workloads against the worst case)
P1 (no behaviour change for valid streams): cache the indirect-arguments page check in the walk,
   as program() already does (`checked`). One cover() per distinct arguments page; the same page
   later in the walk reuses the result (the same point-in-time semantics as the program cache).
   Mesa's D40000 drops from about 224 ms to about 70 ms (13464 reads x 5 us), and the worst case
   for distinct pages is unchanged.
P2 (bound the expensive checks): per walk, at most 8192 uncached page checks (program and
   arguments pages, the old record cap's count). One more is refused as "CDM page budget". Valid
   Mesa streams use a handful of distinct pages. Worst per command = 83 ms reads + 8192 x 4 us =
   about 116 ms.
P3 (bound the SUBMIT): a budget for all CDM walks of one SUBMIT: at most CDM_MAX_BYTES walked and
   8192 page checks in total. Worst per ioctl = about 116 ms. Risk: a SUBMIT with several large
   compute commands that each fit alone could be refused (none seen in dEQP; the
   multi_draw.mosaic stream that motivated the byte budget is one command of about 10k records).
   The alternative is per-SUBMIT 4x, worst about 0.5 s.
My recommendation: P1 + P2 now; P3 only with your agreement. With P1 + P2 the worst case is
about 116 ms per command and about 7.4 s per SUBMIT (from 0.8 s / 51 s), below the old cap's
2.4 s per SUBMIT only if P3 is added. Without P3, 64 maximal barrier streams still take about
5.3 s, which comes from walking 64 x 4 MiB of user memory and which the byte budget allows by
design. Tests (asahi-g15 selftest gate cases): same-page indirect launches make one cover()
(counted by the mock), distinct pages up to the budget are admitted, one past it is refused,
and the existing budget/link cases are unchanged. Then a J516C D40000 timing before and after,
plus the dEQP multi_draw.mosaic cases (pass).

============================================================================================
revision 2: user decision P1 + P2 (8192 page checks per walk). Implemented, tested, measured.
One point needs your call: the 8192 margin (see MARGIN).

CODE (item10.diff; uncommitted on d465423f)
gate.rs:
- struct Checked { program, args: Option<page>, count }.
- check(): one cover() under the budget; ENOSPC once CDM_MAX_PAGE_CHECKS (8192) checks have been
  made.
- program(): its page cache moves into Checked; the check is counted.
- arguments(): new; the 12 argument bytes of an indirect launch are checked once per page and
  cached like the program page. Arguments that cross a page are checked each time and not cached.
- Both launch kinds map ENOSPC to reject("CDM page check budget", va, w), the refusal reason in
  the reject log. The module doc and the constant's doc say why.
asahi-g15 selftest (run.py): the mock cover() counts calls (COVERS) and treats addresses from
1 << 40 on as mapped. New cases:
- repeated_argument_pages_are_checked_once: 1000 launches in one argument page -> 2 checks
  (program page + argument page); alternating between 2 pages -> 11; arguments crossing a page ->
  checked each time.
- page_check_budget_bounds_the_walk: program + 8191 argument pages = 8192 checks, admitted; one
  more page -> refused (EINVAL), and the 8193rd check is not made.
- a_large_stream_of_reused_pages_is_admitted: 170000 launches (4.08 MB, inside the byte budget)
  over 16 pages -> admitted with 17 checks.

VALIDATION
- Fail before: the same tests on the old gate.rs: "FAILED. 71 passed; 3 failed" (1001 instead of
  2; 8193 instead of a refusal at 8192; 170001 instead of 17). After: "ok. 74 passed; 0 failed;
  2 ignored".
- rustfmt (Air, 1.93.1) applied, clean. checkpatch --strict on the diff: 0/0/0. The package
  builds (silicon-12, BUILD-OK); the vmlinux holds "page check budget".
- J516C silicon-12 acceptance (results/20261023-173535-r182-item10-accept): FAIL only on
  crtc_file/presents, as always; kmscube 120.035 fps, EDID 256 bytes, native checks ran.
- Re-measured (results/20261023-r182-item10-cdm-walk/README.md, with the raw before and after
  files, the host benchmark, and gatetime.sh in tools/pb-idle-check):
  - 40000 dispatches: validate 224-229 ms -> 91.6 ms; page checks 41410 -> 6848; reads 13464
    (unchanged).
  - worst case: the P2 bound is analytic (8192 x 4 us + 16640 x 5 us = about 116 ms per
    command, from about 0.8 s). The largest stream the link budget admits through Mesa,
    D47000 at 8047 checks, measured 113.6 ms. D48000 and above are refused by the existing
    64-link budget, not by the page budget (cr 8101 < 8192).
  - dEQP-VK.draw.*multi_draw.mosaic.*.max_draws.*.with_tess.*: 608/608 pass (a superset of the
    3); no CDM rejection or GPU fault lines.

MARGIN (needs your call)
Honeykrisp's indirect-dispatch arguments live in its own upload memory, which moves to a new
16 KiB page about every 7 dispatches. A kprobe on cover() at n=2000 saw 271 argument checks,
71 program checks and 316 distinct pages. So a valid stream's page checks grow with its length,
and the largest stream the link budget admits makes 8050-8100 checks: within about 1% of 8192.
Today the link budget still trips first, but a small change in Mesa's encoding (denser stream
bytes per dispatch, or another pattern) could make a valid stream hit the page budget first. My
recommendation is CDM_MAX_PAGE_CHECKS = 16384 (2x the measured valid maximum). The worst case
becomes 83 ms + 16384 x 4 us = about 149 ms per command and about 9.5 s per 64-command SUBMIT,
against 116 ms and 7.4 s at 8192 and 0.8 s and 51 s before. If you agree, I change the constant
and its test (program + 16383 pages admitted, one more refused) and rerun the selftest and the
D47000 timing. If you keep 8192, I add the measured margin to the commit message and to the
reply to Marcelo.

PLANNED COMMIT: fixup into 1f6f3310 "drm/asahi: g15: m3: bound the CDM stream walk by bytes, not
by 8192 records" (the commit that introduced the walk's current shape). Message: add a paragraph
saying that a page found good (a program's or an indirect launch's arguments) is not checked
again in the walk, and that a walk makes at most N page checks (refused as "CDM page check
budget"). Give the worst cases measured on the J516C: the walk holds no device lock (the queue's
mutex for the SUBMIT, the VM lock per check); page checks about 4 us and window reads about
5 us; a stream of distinct argument pages took about 0.8 s per command before and at most about
116 ms (N=8192) or 149 ms (N=16384) after; 40000 Mesa dispatches 224 -> 92 ms. Add the host-test
lines for the new cases. The subject stays.

